ent (because it has retained children). Also, if the node itself is not removable, we skip.\n\n6. Additionally, we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols are still represented in the remaining tree. That means the set of symbols from all retained nodes must be a superset of required_symbols. So before removing a node, we compute the set of symbols that would be lost (the symbols in the subtree of this node that are not present elsewhere in the retained tree). If any required symbol is only present in this subtree and nowhere else, then we cannot remove it.\n\n7. Since we process bottom-up, we can maintain a global set of symbols present in the current retained tree. Initially, it has all symbols. As we remove nodes, we subtract the symbols of that subtree (but only the symbols that are not also in other retained nodes). To compute efficiently, we can precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree. Then, when we consider removal, we check if the required symbols are present in the set of symbols from all other retained nodes (i.e., global symbols minus those uniquely contributed by this subtree). Actually simpler: we can check if for each required symbol, there is some retained node outside this node's subtree that contributes it. But that's complex.\n\nAlternative: Since the tree is small, we can just simulate removal: tentatively mark the node and its descendants as removed, then check if the remaining nodes (including ancestors and siblings) contain all required symbols. If yes, we keep it removed; otherwise, we revert.\n\nBut we need to process in order and each decision is final. So we can do:\n\n- Maintain a set retained_node_ids (initially all node ids).\n- Maintain a set removed_node_ids (initially empty).\n- Process nodes in order (deepest first, depth descending, then id ascending). For each node:\n   - If node is not removable, skip (cannot remove).\n   - If node is root (id \"root\"), skip (root is never removable, but it's already not removable by flag).\n   - Check if the node has any children that are still retained (i.e., any child whose id is in retained_node_ids). If yes, then we cannot remove this node because it would remove retained children? Actually, the rule says \"never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if any child is retained, skip.\n   - If node has no retained children (all children were already removed or none), then we consider removing this node. Compute the set of symbols in the subtree rooted at this node (including itself and any descendants that are still retained? But if all children are already removed, then subtree is just this node and maybe removed nodes? Actually, if we remove children, they are gone. So the retained subtree of this node is just this node itself. But to check if removing it would lose required symbols, we need to consider the symbols contributed by this node (its own symbol) and any descendants that are still retained? But since we process bottom-up, when we consider a parent, all its children have been either removed or kept. If we kept a child, we cannot remove the parent because it has retained children. So if we are at a parent and all children are removed, then the parent's subtree retained nodes are just the parent itself. So we only need to check if removing this parent would cause loss of a required symbol that is only present in this parent.\n   - Actually, we need to consider that some descendants might be retained if we haven't processed them? But we process deepest first, so deeper nodes are processed before shallower ones. So when we process a node, all its descendants have already been processed. Therefore, if a descendant is retained, it will be a child or deeper, but it's already decided. So the parent's retained children are exactly those children that were not removed. So the condition is correct.\n   - So for a node with no retained children, we can try to remove it. To check if removal is allowed: compute the