\n\nImplementation steps in code:\n\n- Parse case['nodes'] into list.\n- Build dict node_by_id.\n- Build parent-child relationships: for each node, get children (list of nodes with parent==id).\n- Compute depth? Already given.\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending. Use tie-break: id string.\n- Initialize retained_ids = set of all node ids.\n- Initialize removed_ids = [] (order of removal).\n- Initialize symbol_count: dict from symbol to count of nodes with that symbol that are currently retained. Initially count all nodes' symbols.\n- For each node in sorted order:\n   - If node['id'] not in retained_ids: continue (already removed? but we process each once, and removal only happens when we decide, so initially all retained, so this check not needed but safe).\n   - If not node['removable']: continue.\n   - If node['parent'] is None (root): continue (though already not removable).\n   - Check if any child of this node is in retained_ids. If yes, skip (can't remove parent with retained children).\n   - Now compute: node_symbol = node['symbol']\n   - For each required symbol in required_symbols, check if after removal, its count would be >=1. That is, if node_symbol == required and symbol_count[required] == 1, then removal would make count 0, so skip. Actually, if node_symbol is not required, then no required symbol count changes. So check: for any req in required_symbols, if symbol_count[req] - (1 if node_symbol == req else 0) < 1: cannot remove.\n   - If can remove:\n        - removed_ids.append(node['id'])\n        - retained_ids.remove(node['id'])\n        - symbol_count[node_symbol] -= 1\n   - If cannot, keep it.\n\nAfter processing all nodes, we have retained_ids and removed_ids. Then return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": sorted(retained_ids)}? The output spec says arrays of strings. The retained_node_ids should be the nodes that are kept. The order? The spec doesn't specify order for retained, but typically sorted. The removed_node_ids should be in actual removal order. The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted (ascending), removed_node_ids in order of removal (as we appended). We'll do that.\n\nCheck with first fixture case:\n\nNodes: root (depth0), n1 (depth1, symbol s1, removable false), n2 (depth1, s2, removable true), n3 (depth1, s0, removable false), n4 (depth2, parent n2, s1, removable true), n5 (depth2, parent n3, s2, removable false), n6 (depth2, parent n1, s0, removable true). Required: root, s1.\n\nWe process deepest first: depths 2: nodes n4, n5, n6 (depth 2). Order by id: n4, n5, n6 (since ids: n4,n5,n6 alphabetic? Actually id strings: n4, n5, n6 so ascending). \n\n- n4: removable true, parent n2. Check children: none. Symbol s1. Required s1. Count of s1 initially: nodes with s1: n1 (depth1) and n4. So count=2. Removing n4 would reduce s1 count to 1 (since n1 still has s1). So safe. Remove n4. \n- n5: removable false (so skip).\n- n6: removable true, parent n1. Children none. Symbol s0. Required symbols are root and s1, not s0. So removal does not affect required counts. So safe. Remove n6.\n\nNow depth 1: nodes n1, n2, n3 (depth1). Order by id: n1, n2, n3.\n- n1: removable false, skip.\n- n2: removable true, parent root. Check children: n4 was removed, so no retained children. Symbol s2. Required: root, s1. s2 not required. So safe. Remove n2.\n- n3: removable false, skip.\n\nNow depth 0: root, removable false, skip.\n\nResult: removed_node_ids = [n4, n6, n2] (in removal order). retained_node_ids = [root, n1, n3, n5] (since n5 was kept because not removable). Sorted: root, n1, n3, n5. That seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to verify with expected? Not given, but think logically. Also note that we never removed n5 because it's not removable. And we kept n1 because it's not removable. So forest? Actually root has children n1, n2, n3. After removal, n2 gone, so root's children are n1 and n3. n1 has child n6 removed, so n1 has no children. n3 has child n5 kept (n5 is not removable). So final tree: root -> n1, root -> n3 -> n5. Symbols: root (root), n1 (s1), n3 (s0), n5 (s2). Required symbols root and s1 are present. OK.\n\nNow second fixture similar.\n\nThus algorithm works.\n\nBut we must also consider the rule \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" We already enforce that by checking retained children.\n\nAlso, the description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" We do.\n\nAlso, \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" We sort by depth descending then id ascending, so ties broken by id.\n\nNow, we need to implement solve(case) that returns the JSON object. Also handle invalid cases? If required symbols cannot be satisfied, raise ValueError? The description says \"Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" But we assume inputs are valid as per fixture. But we can add a check at the beginning that every required symbol appears in at least one node. If not, raise ValueError.\n\nAlso, we must not mutate case. We'll copy nodes list.\n\nImplementation in Python:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build structures\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children_of = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['parent'] is not None:\n            children_of[n['parent']].append(n['id'])\n    # Check feasibility: every required symbol must be present in at least one node\n    all_symbols = [n['symbol'] for n in nodes]\n    for rs in required_symbols:\n        if rs not in all_symbols:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Required symbol {rs} not found in any node\")\n    # Initialize\n    retained_ids = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    removed_ids = []\n    symbol_count = {}\n    for n in nodes:\n        s = n['symbol']\n        symbol_count[s] = symbol_count.get(s, 0) + 1\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid not in retained_ids:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        if n['parent'] is None:\n            continue\n        # Check if any child is retained\n        has_retained_child = False\n        for cid in children_of.get(nid, []):\n            if cid in retained_ids:\n                has_retained_child = True\n                break\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # Check if removal would lose a required symbol\n        sym = n['symbol']\n        can_remove = True\n        for rs in required_symbols:\n            if symbol_count.get(rs, 0) - (1 if rs == sym else 0) < 1:\n                can_remove = False\n                break\n        if can_remove:\n            removed_ids.append(nid)\n            retained_ids.remove(nid)\n            symbol_count[sym] = symbol_count.get(sym, 0) - 1\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained_ids)\n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n\nBut note: The output contract requires exactly these two keys. Also, we must return a JSON-serializable object, which this dict is.\n\nNow, we need to output the implementation as a JSON string inside {\"implementation\": \"...\"}. So we need to escape the Python code.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer.\n\nMake sure to handle the case where node has no parent (root). Also, children_of might not have entry for root? We initialized for all ids, so ok.\n\nWe'll also add a check for duplicate ids? Not needed.\n\nNow, write the response."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2189,"completion_tokens":4919,"total_tokens":7108,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2176},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4419},"prompt_cache_